# Test: a refused edit answers a fragment, htmx 4 swaps it, and the toast repeats it
# VALIDATES: POST /config/set/ refused at 400 answers markup; htmx 4 swaps every
# response but 204 and 304, so the fragment reaches the field and the
# out-of-band item reaches the error drawer; and assets/notification.js still
# reads the sentence out of that markup and shows it as a toast.
# PREVENTS: a failed action with no feedback. Under htmx 2 the toast was the
# only feedback a refusal had, because htmx 2 swapped no 4xx. htmx 4 swaps it,
# so the page now carries the message twice over, and a toast reading the RAW
# markup instead of its sentence is the regression this still catches.

option=timeout:value=60s

action=open:path=/show/system/dns/
expect=element:id=input-system-sdns-stimeout

# 70000 is one above the uint16 ceiling, so config.ValidateValue refuses it and
# the handler answers 400.
#
# Enter is what sends it. The field also posts on "input changed delay:1s", and
# waiting for that debounce alone made this test spend its whole 15s expectation
# budget under load: initEnterSubmit (cli.js) dispatches ze-enter, which the
# input's hx-trigger names, so the post leaves on the keystroke instead.
action=fill:id=input-system-sdns-stimeout:value=70000
action=press:key=Enter
action=wait:ms=2000

# The toast an operator reads. The message is the handler's sentence, not the
# markup around it, which is what errorMessageFromBody (notification.js) buys.
# The value the operator typed is in that sentence, and the class name is not:
# a toast built from the raw body would carry error-fragment as text.
expect=element:text=Error 400
expect=element:text=invalid uint16
expect=element:text=70000
expect=element:not-text=error-fragment

# htmx 4 swaps a 4xx, so both halves of the body reach the page: the fragment
# into the field's own target, and the out-of-band item into the error list.
# Under htmx 2 neither did, and the toast stood alone.
expect=html:contains=error-fragment
expect=html:contains=error-item
